  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 320 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nSlightly cooler, slightly less humid conditions remain expected \nThursday through Sunday with highs generally ranging from the low \n80s north of the Atlanta metro to the low 90s across our far \nsoutheastern counties, and lows ranging from the upper 60s north to \nlower 70s southeast. The most noticeable relief from our recent heat \nwill definitely be felt across the north Georgia mountains, with \nless of a real pattern change as you head south. Variable areas of \neven cooler conditions will be possible tied to thunderstorms and \nattendant cloud cover. The heat and humidity look to return in \nearnest early next week, with high temperatures rising back into the \nmid to upper 90s and heat index values reaching 100 north to 110 \nsoutheast. If this forecast holds, Heat Advisories may be needed for \nportions of the CWA as early as Monday.\n\nMeanwhile, the weak cool front responsible for the modest relief \nlate week into the weekend is forecast to stall/wash out across the \narea, and combine with additional moisture from Tropical Storm \nBertha (or its remnants) to continue to support increased daily \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms. We now carry PoPs of 50-80% \nacross large swaths of the CWA each day through Saturday. Localized \nflash flooding will continue to be a primary concern through this \nperiod with model forecast soundings suggesting PWATs of 2.00 to \n2.50 inches and relatively weak steering flow at times, with this \nrisk locally enhanced in locations that become saturated from a \nprevious day's storms. WPC currently carries a Marginal Risk to \nSlight Risk of excessive rainfall across the entire CWA Thursday, \nwith a Marginal Risk continuing for most areas Friday and Saturday. \nGusty winds will remain possible with the stronger storms as well. \nStorm coverage will return to a more typical isolated to scattered \nposture early next week as the heat returns.",
